The translation is a single [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m exporting the two symbols the C object actually defines — [38;5;10msiphash[0m and [38;5;10mstbds_hash_bytes[0m (the latter is non-[38;5;10mstatic[0m in the C, so it's part of the ABI even though only [38;5;10msiphash[0m appears in [38;5;10mlib.h[0m). There are no namespace-renaming macros in the header, so the linker names are unprefixed; [38;5;10mnm[0m confirms both.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three details drove the implementation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **Signed-int byte gathering.** In C, [38;5;10md[0] | (d[1]<<8) | (d[2]<<16) | (d[3]<<24)[0m is evaluated in [38;5;10mint[0m because [38;5;10munsigned char[0m promotes. When [38;5;10md[3] >= 0x80[0m the result is a negative [38;5;10mint[0m and the implicit conversion to [38;5;10msize_t[0m sign-extends, flooding the top 32 bits of [38;5;10mdata[0m with ones. That happens in the main loop and again at [38;5;10mcase 4:[0m of the tail switch. I reproduced it with [38;5;10mi32[0m arithmetic plus a sign-extending [38;5;10mas u64[0m cast rather than treating it as unsigned. The high-half gather has the same sign extension, but [38;5;10m<< 16 << 16[0m shifts the extension bits out, so it behaves unsigned.[0m[0m
- **Switch fallthrough.** The tail [38;5;10mswitch (len - i)[0m falls through from the matching case down to [38;5;10mcase 0[0m, so every case from 1 up to [38;5;10mlen - i[0m executes. All are [38;5;10m|=[0m, so I used ascending [38;5;10mif rem >= k[0m guards — equivalent, including the sign-extension case, since later ORs can't clear those bits.[0m[0m
- **Output path.** [38;5;10msiphash[0m prints via C [38;5;10mprintf[0m with the identical format strings, so stdio buffering and any interleaving with a C caller's own output stay identical. Using Rust's [38;5;10mprint![0m would have introduced a separate buffer.[0m[0m
